@@s035 Calculated with the course rule ("Tuesday 10:00, one week after the lecture", "Sunday 23:59, two weeks after"). Which 10 of about 13 sets are assessed is not yet published: LearnIT is the reference.
@@s036 The work cycle
@@s037 How to work on an exercise sheet
@@s038 Open a terminal in the week's folder. <code>-w</code> reruns the tests on every save.
@@s039 Open <code>Exercises.scala</code> and replace one <code>???</code> at a time.
@@s040 Save and look at the terminal: the test of that exercise must go from red to green.
@@s041 The course README: when a test fails, the first thing is <b>to read the test</b>. Almost always it tells you what it expects.
@@s042 If you cannot finish an exercise
@@s043 <b>Do not submit code that does not compile</b>: the whole file would be worth zero, even if the other exercises were right. Leave <code>???</code> (it compiles, it fails only that test) or comment out the lines that do not compile.
@@s044 Reading the test output
@@s045 Real output, obtained by running the week 1 tests on a copy with two deliberate errors: <code>square</code> left at <code>???</code> and <code>fib</code> starting from <code>(1, 1)</code> instead of <code>(0, 1)</code>.
@@s046 Line by line
@@s047 <code>+</code> = test passed. <code>Ex04.12</code> = exercise 4, test number 12. "passed 100 tests": ScalaCheck tried 100 random inputs.
@@s048 Test with a fixed input (no randomness): it passes or it does not.
@@s049 <code>!</code> = failed. "Falsified": your function gives a <b>wrong</b> result. The title of the test says what it expects: the fourth Fibonacci must be 2.
@@s050 The test exploded with an exception. <code>NotImplementedError</code> = you left <code>???</code>. It is not a logic error: you only have to write the exercise.
@@s051 The input that made the test fail. <code>ARG_0_ORIGINAL</code> is the one found first; <code>ARG_0</code> is the simplest version that still fails (ScalaCheck "shrinks" the input). Use <code>ARG_0</code> to try it in the REPL.
@@s052 The "seed" of the randomness: it serves to reproduce exactly the same test. Usually you can ignore it.
@@s053 The summary: 3 failed + 1 with exception = 4 tests not passed. With at most 5 the homework would pass anyway, but only if the whole file compiles.
@@s054 "It worked before, now it does not"
@@s055 <b>Compile the whole file, not only the exercise you are working on.</b> An error in exercise 9 also blocks the tests of exercise 1. Look at the first compiler error, not the last.
@@s056 <b>Dirty cache</b>: <code>scala-cli clean .</code> and then <code>scala-cli test .</code> again.
@@s057 <b>Wrong imports</b>: from week 3 the library lists are used, from week 4 the course's LazyList. The file already has the right imports: do not add others.
@@s058 Asking for help
@@s059 The course Discord (not email). You can paste code fragments into the question. The TAs give oral feedback in the exercise sessions, or written feedback on LearnIT to anyone who asks for it.
